CertHub.addPlanDetail("ai-102", {"study":{"1":[["When would you choose a multi-service Azure AI services resource over single-service resources?","When one app uses several services and you want one endpoint, one key and one bill. Single-service resources let you pick a Free tier per service, separate billing and keys per service, and limit what each key can call."],["Why prefer Microsoft Entra ID authentication over API keys?","Keys are shared secrets that anyone holding them can use, and they must be stored and rotated. Entra ID with a managed identity avoids secrets in code, gives per-identity RBAC (for example Cognitive Services User) and shows who called in logs. It needs a custom subdomain endpoint."],["How do you rotate an Azure AI services key without downtime?","Each resource has two keys. Move apps to key 2, regenerate key 1, then later move apps back to key 1 and regenerate key 2. Storing the key in Key Vault makes the switch a single secret update."],["What three settings does an Azure AI services container need to start, and why?","ApiKey, Billing (the resource endpoint) and Eula=accept. Connected containers report usage to the Azure resource for billing; they do not send your text or images. Disconnected containers need Microsoft approval and a commitment plan."],["Name the four harm categories in Azure AI Content Safety and how severity works.","Hate, sexual, violence and self-harm. Each result carries a severity level, and you choose the threshold at which content is blocked or sent for review; custom blocklists catch specific terms the categories would not."],["What do prompt shields and groundedness detection protect against?","Prompt shields detect user prompt attacks (jailbreaks) and indirect attacks hidden in documents or tool results. Groundedness detection checks whether a model's answer is supported by the source material you supply, flagging ungrounded claims."]],"2":[["What does each message role do in a chat completion?","The system message sets behavior, persona, rules and format. User messages carry the user's input. Assistant messages hold earlier model replies, and replaying them gives the model conversation history, because each request is stateless."],["Temperature vs top_p?","Both control randomness. Temperature reshapes the probability distribution (low = focused, repeatable; high = varied). top_p limits sampling to the smallest set of tokens whose probability adds up to p. Adjust one, not both."],["Why use RAG instead of fine-tuning to answer questions about company documents?","RAG retrieves current passages at query time and puts them in the prompt, so answers stay up to date, can cite sources and need no retraining. Fine-tuning changes style and behavior but is a poor way to add changing facts."],["Why chunk documents before creating embeddings?","Embedding models and the chat model's context window have token limits, and small focused chunks retrieve more precisely. Chunks usually overlap slightly so a sentence split across a boundary is not lost."],["What does a groundedness evaluation measure, and why does it matter for RAG?","Whether the answer's claims are supported by the retrieved context. A low score means the model is adding information that is not in your sources, which is how hallucinations appear in RAG apps."],["What causes HTTP 429 responses from an Azure OpenAI deployment and how do you handle them?","The deployment exceeded its tokens-per-minute or requests-per-minute limit. Retry with exponential backoff honoring the retry-after header, raise the deployment's quota, spread load across deployments, or use provisioned throughput."]],"3":[["What makes an agent different from a chat completion call?","An agent combines a model, instructions and tools, and runs a loop: it decides which tool to call, uses the result and continues until the task is done, keeping conversation state in a thread. A plain chat call only returns text."],["Explain threads, messages and runs in Azure AI Foundry Agent Service.","A thread is a conversation that stores messages. You add a user message to the thread, then create a run that asks the agent to process the thread; the run moves through statuses such as queued, in_progress, requires_action and completed."],["What does the requires_action run status mean?","The agent wants your code to execute a function it has called. Your app reads the tool calls, runs the functions, and submits the tool outputs so the run can continue."],["Code interpreter vs file search?","Code interpreter lets the agent write and run Python in a sandbox to calculate, transform files and draw charts. File search chunks and embeds uploaded files in a vector store and retrieves relevant passages to ground answers."],["How do you keep an agent safe when it can take actions?","Give each tool the least privilege it needs, require human approval before irreversible actions, validate tool inputs, apply content filters and prompt shields, and trace runs so every tool call can be reviewed."]],"4":[["Caption vs dense captions vs tags?","Caption gives one sentence describing the whole image. Dense captions give sentences for up to several regions, each with a bounding box. Tags are single words for things, scenery and actions with confidence scores."],["Multiclass vs multilabel classification in Custom Vision?","Multiclass assigns exactly one tag per image. Multilabel lets an image have any number of tags. Neither gives locations; for locations use object detection."],["What do precision and recall tell you about a Custom Vision iteration?","Precision is the share of predictions for a tag that were correct. Recall is the share of actual instances that the model found. Raising the probability threshold usually increases precision and lowers recall."],["How do you run a Custom Vision model offline on a device?","Train with a compact domain, then export the iteration in a format such as TensorFlow, ONNX, Core ML or a Docker container. Non-compact domains cannot be exported."],["What Face features are Limited Access?","Face identification and verification (recognizing who someone is) require an application and approval from Microsoft. Detection and many quality attributes are generally available, and emotion, gender and age inference were retired."]],"5":[["Entity recognition vs entity linking?","Named entity recognition finds and categorizes entities such as people, places and dates. Entity linking resolves an entity to a specific entry in a knowledge base, telling apart meanings such as Mars the planet and Mars the company."],["Which headers does a Translator text request need with a regional resource?","Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key with the key and Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Region with the resource's region (also needed for multi-service resources). Target languages are passed as one or more `to` query parameters."],["When do you use batch transcription instead of real-time speech to text?","For large numbers of prerecorded audio files, such as call recordings in Blob Storage, processed asynchronously. Real-time recognition is for live microphone or streaming audio."],["Name three SSML elements and what they do.","<prosody> changes rate, pitch and volume; <break> inserts a pause; <say-as> controls how dates, numbers or characters are read; <phoneme> sets pronunciation; <mstts:express-as> applies a speaking style for supported voices."],["What is the None intent in CLU for?","It catches utterances that are outside the app's domain. Adding examples to it stops the model forcing unrelated input into a real intent."],["How does custom question answering handle follow-up questions?","With multi-turn follow-up prompts attached to an answer, which offer the next questions and link to other question-answer pairs, so a conversation can narrow down to the right answer."]],"6":[["What does each Azure AI Search component do?","A data source points at the data. An indexer crawls it on a schedule, cracks documents and maps fields. A skillset runs AI enrichment during indexing. The index stores searchable content. The knowledge store optionally saves enriched output to Azure Storage."],["Replicas vs partitions?","Replicas are copies of the index that handle queries and give high availability; add them for query throughput and the SLA. Partitions split storage and indexing; add them for larger indexes. Billing is based on search units (replicas × partitions)."],["Which field attributes support filters, facets and sorting?","filterable for $filter, facetable for facet counts, sortable for $orderby, searchable for full-text search and retrievable for returning the field in results. Each attribute adds storage."],["What is hybrid search and why does it help RAG?","It runs a keyword (BM25) query and a vector query together and merges the results with Reciprocal Rank Fusion. Vectors catch meaning and paraphrases; keywords catch exact terms such as product codes. Semantic ranking can re-rank the merged results."],["Template vs neural custom models in Document Intelligence?","Template models suit forms with a fixed layout and train quickly. Neural models handle structured and semi-structured documents whose layout varies, such as invoices from many vendors, and are the usual first choice."],["What is a composed model?","Several custom models grouped under one model ID. Document Intelligence classifies each incoming document and routes it to the best-matching model, so the app calls a single model for many form types."]]},"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Plan and manage an Azure AI solution","w":24,"topics":["Choosing the right Azure AI service for a workload: Azure OpenAI, AI Search, Vision, Language, Speech, Translator and Document Intelligence","Azure AI Foundry hubs, projects and resources vs single-service and multi-service Azure AI services resources","Planning model deployments: regions, model availability, deployment types (Standard, Global, Data Zone, Provisioned) and quotas","Deploying Azure AI resources with the portal, Azure CLI, ARM templates and Bicep","Endpoints, keys and SDKs: calling Azure AI services with REST and the Python and C# SDKs","Authentication: API keys vs Microsoft Entra ID, managed identities and Cognitive Services RBAC roles","Protecting keys and networks: Azure Key Vault, key rotation, private endpoints and network restrictions","Running Azure AI services in containers: connected and disconnected containers and billing settings","Monitoring and cost: Azure Monitor metrics, diagnostic logs, alerts, pricing tiers and budgets","Responsible AI principles and Azure AI Content Safety: harm categories, severity levels and blocklists","Content filters, prompt shields and groundedness detection for generative AI applications"],"notes":["Skills measured: Select the appropriate Azure AI Foundry services; Plan, create and deploy an Azure AI Foundry service; Manage, monitor and secure an Azure AI Foundry service; Implement AI solutions responsibly"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Language resource on the Free (F0) tier, copy its endpoint and key, and call sentiment analysis with curl or the Python SDK. Then regenerate key 1 and confirm the old key fails while key 2 still works. Cost warning: stay on F0 and delete the resource group when done.","Free Azure account: give your own user the Cognitive Services User role on the Language resource, set a custom subdomain if the portal asks, and call the API with a Microsoft Entra ID token from `az account get-access-token` instead of a key. Store the key as a secret in a Key Vault you create, then delete the resource group afterward.","Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Content Safety resource on F0, test text and images in the Content Safety try-it pages in Azure AI Foundry, add a custom blocklist with two terms and see a post get flagged. Add a diagnostic setting to a Log Analytics workspace, then delete everything so nothing keeps billing after your free credit."]},{"id":2,"name":"Implement generative AI solutions","w":18,"topics":["Azure OpenAI and the Azure AI Foundry model catalog: chat, reasoning, embedding and image models, and how to choose and deploy one","Chat completions: system, user and assistant messages, and calling a deployment with the Azure OpenAI SDK","Tuning model output with parameters: temperature, top_p, max tokens, stop sequences and penalties","Prompt engineering techniques: clear instructions, few-shot examples, output formats and step-by-step reasoning","Retrieval augmented generation (RAG): grounding a model in your own data with Azure AI Search","Embeddings and vector retrieval for RAG: chunking documents, embedding models and similarity search","Prompt flow in Azure AI Foundry: flows, nodes, connections, variants and deployment","Evaluating generative AI apps: groundedness, relevance, coherence, fluency and safety evaluations","Fine-tuning vs prompt engineering vs RAG: when each approach fits and how fine-tuning data is prepared","Generating images and working with multimodal chat models that accept images","Operating generative AI apps: token usage, rate limits, latency, tracing and monitoring"],"notes":["Skills measured: Build generative AI solutions with Azure AI Foundry; Use Azure OpenAI in Foundry Models to generate content; Optimize and operationalize a generative AI solution"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Foundry project, deploy a small chat model with a low tokens-per-minute limit, and use the chat playground to change the system message, temperature and max tokens, noting how the answers change. Cost warning: model calls are billed per token, so keep it to a few dozen prompts within your free credit and delete the deployment afterward.","Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Search service on the Free tier, upload three short text files to a storage container, and use the 'add your data' option in the chat playground to ground answers in them. Ask a question the files answer and one they don't. Delete the search service, storage account and deployment when done.","Free Azure account: from Python, call your chat deployment with the openai package's AzureOpenAI client, print the `usage` token counts and `finish_reason`, then set a tiny max tokens value to see `length` returned. Call an embedding deployment on two similar sentences and compare the vectors with cosine similarity. Delete the deployments afterward."]},{"id":3,"name":"Implement an agentic solution","w":8,"topics":["What an AI agent is: model, instructions and tools, and when an agent fits better than a plain chat app","Azure AI Foundry Agent Service: agents, threads, messages and runs","Agent tools: file search, code interpreter, Azure AI Search, OpenAPI and function calling","Function calling: describing functions with JSON schema and handling tool calls in code","Building agents in code with Semantic Kernel, AutoGen and the Microsoft Agent Framework","Multi-agent solutions: orchestration patterns, handoffs and connected agents","Testing, securing and monitoring agents: tracing, human approval and least-privilege tools","Deploying agents and integrating them into applications"],"notes":["Skills measured: Create custom agents (agent roles and use cases, Azure AI Foundry Agent Service, Semantic Kernel and AutoGen, multi-agent orchestration, testing and deployment)"],"labs":["Free Azure account: in your Azure AI Foundry project open the Agents page, create an agent on a small chat model with short instructions, attach the code interpreter tool, upload a small CSV and ask it for a summary and a chart. Look at the thread and run details. Cost warning: token and tool usage are billed, so keep it short and delete the agent and deployment afterward.","Free Azure account: create a second agent with the file search tool over two short policy documents, ask questions whose answers are and are not in the files, and check which file the citation points to. Delete the vector store, files and agent when you finish.","Free (local): write a short Python script that defines a `get_weather(city)` function and its JSON schema, send it to your chat deployment as a tool, and print the tool call the model returns before running the function yourself and sending the result back. Only the few model calls cost anything; delete the deployment when done."]},{"id":4,"name":"Implement computer vision solutions","w":13,"topics":["Azure AI Vision Image Analysis: captions, dense captions, tags, objects, people and smart crops","Reading printed and handwritten text with the OCR (Read) feature of Azure AI Vision","Custom Vision: image classification (multiclass vs multilabel) vs object detection","Training and evaluating a Custom Vision model: tagging images, iterations, precision, recall and mAP","Publishing and consuming a Custom Vision model: prediction resource, published iterations and exporting compact models","Azure AI Face: face detection, attributes and Limited Access features","Analyzing video with Azure AI Video Indexer","Choosing between prebuilt Image Analysis, Custom Vision and multimodal models for a vision task"],"notes":["Skills measured: Analyze images; Implement custom computer vision models; Analyze videos"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Vision resource on F0 and, in Vision Studio or with the Python SDK, run Image Analysis on three of your own photos with the caption, tags, objects and read features. Compare the confidence scores. Stay on F0 and delete the resource afterward.","Free Azure account: create Custom Vision training and prediction resources on F0, build a multiclass classifier with two tags (for example mugs and bottles) from photos you take, train, check precision and recall, publish the iteration and test it with the prediction endpoint. Delete both resources afterward.","Free Azure account: create an object detection project on F0 in Custom Vision, draw bounding boxes on at least 15 images per tag, train, and adjust the probability threshold to see precision and recall change. Export is only offered for compact domains, so note which domain you chose. Delete the project and resources when done."]},{"id":5,"name":"Implement natural language processing solutions","w":18,"topics":["Azure AI Language text analysis: language detection, key phrases, entities, entity linking and sentiment with opinion mining","Detecting and redacting personally identifiable information (PII) with Azure AI Language","Translating text and documents with Azure AI Translator and Custom Translator","Speech to text with Azure AI Speech: real-time, continuous and batch transcription","Text to speech with neural voices and SSML for pronunciation, pauses, rate and style","Speech translation, intent recognition and keyword recognition with the Speech SDK","Conversational language understanding (CLU): intents, entities, utterances, training and deployment","Custom question answering: projects, sources, multi-turn prompts, synonyms and confidence thresholds","Custom text classification and custom named entity recognition","Custom speech models and custom neural voice: when to train them and responsible use limits"],"notes":["Skills measured: Analyze and translate text; Process and translate speech; Implement custom language models"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Language resource on F0 and, in Language Studio, run sentiment analysis with opinion mining and PII detection on a few made-up customer reviews that contain fake names and phone numbers. Look at the redacted text. Delete the resource when done.","Free Azure account: create a Speech resource on F0 and use the Python Speech SDK to transcribe your microphone once, then synthesize a sentence with SSML that uses <prosody rate> and <break>. Create a Translator resource on F0 and translate one sentence into two languages in a single REST call. Delete the resources afterward.","Free Azure account: in Language Studio build a small conversational language understanding project with three intents and a list entity, add about ten utterances per intent, train, test and deploy it, then query the deployment. Build a custom question answering project from a short FAQ file too (it needs an Azure AI Search resource: use the Free tier). Delete everything afterward."]},{"id":6,"name":"Implement knowledge mining and information extraction solutions","w":19,"topics":["Azure AI Search components: data sources, indexers, indexes, skillsets, service tiers, replicas and partitions","Designing a search index: key field, field attributes (searchable, filterable, sortable, facetable, retrievable), analyzers and suggesters","AI enrichment with built-in skills: document cracking, OCR, image analysis, entities and key phrases in a skillset","Custom skills: calling an Azure Function or web API from a skillset","Querying an index: simple and full Lucene syntax, OData filters, facets, sorting and paging","Semantic ranking, vector search and hybrid queries in Azure AI Search","Knowledge store: projections to tables, objects and files in Azure Storage","Azure AI Document Intelligence prebuilt models: read, layout, invoice, receipt and ID document","Custom Document Intelligence models: template vs neural, custom classifiers and composed models","Azure AI Content Understanding: extracting fields from documents, images, audio and video with analyzers"],"notes":["Skills measured: Implement an Azure AI Search solution; Implement an Azure AI Document Intelligence solution; Extract information with Azure AI Content Understanding"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Search service on the Free tier and a storage account, upload a few PDFs, and run the Import data wizard with OCR, key phrase and entity skills. The free enrichment allowance covers a handful of documents. Query the index in Search explorer with a filter and a facet. Delete the search service and storage account afterward.","Free Azure account: create a Document Intelligence resource on F0 and, in Document Intelligence Studio, analyze a receipt photo with the prebuilt receipt model and a multi-column page with the layout model. Compare fields, tables and confidence scores. The free tier has page limits, so use short files, and delete the resource when done.","Free Azure account: in Document Intelligence Studio, label five copies of a simple form you made yourself (name, date, amount), train a custom template model on F0 and test it on a sixth copy. Then add a knowledge store with a table projection to your search skillset and open the table in Storage Browser. Delete all resources afterward."]}]});
